## Availability / precision conclusions

| backend | available | clock API (underlying)                  | two diff readings (us) | observed resolution (min non-zero, 1000 samples) |
|---------|-----------|------------------------------------------|------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|
| native  | YES       | extern "C" moonbit_monotonic_clock_start/stop | 0, 0              | ~0.1 us (100 ns)                                  |
| js      | YES       | performance.now() via extern "js"        | 8.2, 0.3               | ~0.1 us (100 ns); node v26.7.0 confirmed working (E3) |
| wasm    | YES*      | WASI instant_now/instant_elapsed_as_secs_f64 | 40.6, 1.7          | ~0.3 us                                           |

Notes:
- diff1/diff2 = elapsed us of two consecutive monotonic clock reads
  (pair-read overhead + resolution). Zero readings on native mean two
  consecutive reads landed inside one clock tick — expected for a 100 ns
  resolution clock; min_nonzero over 1000 samples gives the true resolution.
- *wasm: moon 0.1.20260904 on this host LAUNCHED the compiled wasm probe
  successfully (exit 0) and produced real readings. This CONTRADICTS the
  earlier host note "wasm cannot launch on this host" recorded in
  tests/diffstats/main.mbt (that observation was made for the diffstats
  reporter under an earlier toolchain state). Recorded explicitly per the
  no-silent-skip rule; if a future run fails to launch wasm, the failure
  message must be recorded here instead of readings.
- js: monotonicity source is performance.now() (Node hrtime-backed), not
  Date.now(); node itself confirmed available (v26.7.0).

## Fallback design (registered, NOT implemented in T-01)

All three backends compile and run against moonbitlang/core/bench's
monotonic clock pair, so no fallback was activated. Directions registered
for future use, to be converged INSIDE the seam function (per-backend
clock_*.mbt files) if a backend ever lacks the monotonic pair:
  js:   extern "js" Date.now() — millisecond wall-clock fallback (coarse,
        non-monotonic; last resort only)
  wasm: host-injected clock / WASI clock probing

